## §1 — OBSERVE

- **Queue state (read this cycle):** `hidden_files/study-jobs/QUEUE.md` RECREATION QUEUE — T218 stands at row #100 (Packet #12), status OWED/DESCRIBED at Wave 35 close ("DESCRIBED-OWED 6 (rows #100–#105: T218 · T219 · T227 · T232 · T233 · T238)"), first attempt owned by this job (A3 §3). The wave RUN.md §0b census (seat, firsthand) concurs.
- **Consumer (named, §1.4):** the **DLC assembler diagram container** — the block-weighted count-board surface owned by the Daily Language Challenge assembler lane (packet §2: "Primary consumer: Hadosh Academy explainer production + the DLC assembler — diagram container"; style-library §T218: "Feeds: diagram"). The machinery lifts as a diagram container that holds a tile representation still while the counting RULE changes (free population → + enslaved population), so the rule is what the viewer sees change.

### 1.1 Catalogue mechanics (read firsthand)
- Digest §T17: "State tiles arranged in two labelled camps (South pro-slavery / North anti-slavery); arrows move population between categories as the narration explains that only free people were proposed to count, and the South wanted enslaved people counted (transcript 4:37–4:55)." Why it works: the founding bargain is drawn with the same tile grammar as the modern data (T16) — present and origin share one visual language. Evidence: ev/t282-north-south-tiles.jpg (282 s).
- Style-library techniques-diagrams.md §T218: agrees (mechanics, window P12 @ 4:31–5:05, evidence, consumer). Operation named there: "tile move as re-categorisation." Tier DESCRIBED — OWED #100. No digest↔register disagreement.
- Transcript anchors (sentences.json + words.json): 270.77 "Back when there were just a few states, not 50…" · 276.98 "The Northern states, which were largely anti-slavery, wanted only free people to count in the population towards electoral votes." (276.98–284.7) · 284.97 "Which they had more of." · 286.81 "The pro-slavery Southern states were worried that they would be constantly outvoted, and wanted enslaved people to count in determining the population." (286.81–294.9) · 295.59 "As a compromise, they settled on something called the 'three-fifths clause'." — the last sentence is T219's subject, spoken over this window's tail.
- Packet gaps: Packet #12 adopted WITH GAPS — G3 (clip-filename offset) and G4 (mechanics thinness on T02/T05/T06/T10 packet-local) do not attach to T17/T218 (confirmed by codex-observe, §2b). The census below is the firsthand cure for the digest's one-line mechanics, which compresses two re-categorisation acts (free sizing, then enslaved framing) into the word "arrows."

### 1.2 Evidence frame viewed firsthand
ev/t282-north-south-tiles.jpg (282 s, sha256 8c1b1e4f…c4d2): the FREE-population state — 12 dark teal-grey tiles in two camps under serif headers "South / Pro-slavery" (left) and "North / Anti-slavery" (right); tile AREA varies widely (Pennsylvania, Virginia, Massachusetts, New York largest; Delaware, Georgia smallest); each tile carries a darker state silhouette and a white serif state name; centre label "Free people population (mostly white)" with two teal curved arrows pointing down-left (to North Carolina) and down-right (to New York) — one arrow per camp. NOTE: at this instant the tiles are at their free-population sizes and NO enslaved frame exists yet — the frame act arrives ≈10 s later (census CP11). The digest's "arrows move population between categories" is the film's label-and-arrow device for announcing each counting rule, not a literal tile migration: no tile ever changes camp in the census.

